## Deal Highlight: Timely Intro Saves a Steel Contractor From a Costly Bank Exit

When their incumbent bank abruptly terminated a $500K line of credit, this Georgia steel framing contractor was given four months to repay the balance. For a company generating $5MM in annual revenue, that is an extremely difficult ask. The owner turned to a second local bank, but a minor loss on the company's tax return disqualified them from traditional financing.

That second banker didn't stop there. He made the introduction to Serve Funding.

We moved swiftly, securing a $550K unsecured term loan to cover the first portion of the bank payoff and key vendor payables. A few months later we delivered an additional $250K, letting the client fully resolve the outstanding balance. The banker who made the introduction earned the client's deposit and treasury relationship a few months later, and the contractor now views both as long-term advisors.

The full story, including what came next, is in the case study: [$550K in Bridge Capital for a Steel Framing Contractor](/blog/steel-contractor-bank-exit).

{% callout type="tip" title="The takeaway" %}
A well-placed introduction helped the client stay afloat, gave the new bank a relationship it would not otherwise have earned, and gave Serve Funding the chance to deliver strategic capital over time. Everyone wins, together.
{% /callout %}

## More Than Money: Core Value of the Month, Responsibility

We don't just fund deals. We own outcomes.

Our team remains accountable to the client, and to everyone else at the table, throughout the entire funding process. That means:

- Full transparency with the business owner and the advisors around them
- Timely updates and documentation
- No cross-selling. The relationships a business already has stay exactly where they are.

We follow through so that anyone who introduces us can do it with confidence. That's what responsibility means to us.
